  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n- Rain showers and cooling this afternoon\n\nRadar shows rain showers crossing southwest Lower MI at the \nleading edge of a cold front. Satellite IR trends indicate a \ndecrease in coverage of coldest cloud top temperatures with slight\nwarming. Correspondingly, radar shows precipitation to be a \nlittle less intense and extensive in coverage; nonetheless, we \nexpect the entire forecast area to pick up measurable rainfall \nthis afternoon. \n\nAs noted previously, we can expect falling temperatures this \nafternoon. Temperatures over western WI at this time are in the \nlower 40s, indicating appreciable cold advection behind the front. \nAlso behind the front, the Airmass RGB satellite product shows deep \ntropospheric subsidence that should give our area a chance for at \nleast some clearing overnight. Any cloudiness would probably be lake \neffect and more likely to be extensive near the lakeshore. \n\n\n- Brief and non-impactful light snow showers Monday\n\nModel guidance continues to support non-impactful accumulating snow \nbeginning Monday afternoon and lasting into early Monday night. The\nbest estimate for timing, based on most recently available Bufkit\nguidance, is between noon and 5 PM. This will be when an \neffectively saturated DGZ humidity and lift will be passing \nthrough, continuing the model trend of favorable DGZ moistening \nthat was mentioned in the previous discussion. Snow accumulations \nwill be limited due to a high DGZ and substantial lift in a less \nhumid sub-cloud layer, leading to riming that could completely \nmelt the flakes in areas farther south and inland. The US 10 \ncorridor could see farther inland accumulations however. As noted \nbefore, pavement temperatures may be a touch too warm for much in \nthe way of slippery roads, especially since at least some downwelling\nof IR radiation from clouds can be expected at this time.\n\n\n- Rain mixed with snow from Thanksgiving into the weekend\n\nThe overarching expectation from Thanksgiving into the weekend is \nfor a mix of rain and snow. This will mostly be rain due to forecast \nhighs well into the 40s. This forecast is of course subject to \nchange with a 5 to 10 percent chance of impactful snow. \n\nDeterministic guidance, particularly the ECWMF, has been much more \naggressive with QPF and snow potential compared to the ensemble \nmember solutions. The law of averages would tell us that the \ndeterministic QPF would be at or above the 90th percentile about a \ntenth of the time. However, for whatever reason, we are seeing this \nbehavior very consistently, for at least the last 3 cycles now. This \nundoubtedly is feeding concerns for impactful Thanksgiving weather, \nbut the more trusted ensemble solution space instead shows a slow \nand steady increase in QPF that, for now, still looks unimpactful. \nFor example, the latest available NBM indicates a less than 50 \npercent chance of accumulating half an inch of rain for this system.\n\nSo, the majority report at this point favors generally unimpressive \nweather for the weekend. However, predictability at this range \nremains poor, especially given that a phasing and cyclonically \nbreaking southern stream trough late in the period could be a major \nfactor, or non-factor. For this reason, impactful weather cannot be \ncompletely ruled out.",
